WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 9: Lights, Camera, Action….

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:05.920
Welcome to Unit 9!

3
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:12.320
This unit is all about drama and scripts — the
special kinds of writing used for plays and films.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.820
When actors perform on a stage or in
front of a camera, they follow a script.

5
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.820
A script is very different from a story in a book.

6
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:25.120
Instead of long descriptions, a script
uses dialogue (the words characters say),

7
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:32.740
stage directions (instructions for the actors), and sometimes
camera directions (instructions for how to film a scene).

8
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.420
In this unit, you will learn how to read,
understand, and write your own play scripts.

9
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.160
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.760
By the end of this unit, you will be able to
recognise the layout and features of a play script,

11
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.020
including character names, colons,
dialogue, and stage directions.

12
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.500
you will also explain the difference
between a play script and a film script.

13
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.560
you will also read stage directions and understand terms
like upstage, downstage, centre stage, and exits/enters.

14
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.000
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

15
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:13.240
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
nook, woodland, bamboo, groove, fertile, textbook.

16
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.260
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

17
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.880
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

18
00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:33.820
The reading sequence includes Lights, Camera, Action…:
source text 1 and Lights, Camera, Action…: source text 2.

19
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.100
Read actively.

20
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.040
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.620
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.060
The first language focus is What Are Compound Nouns?.

23
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:56.060
What it means and why it matters A compound noun is a noun
made from two or more words combined together to create a new

24
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:56.460
meaning.

25
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.060
Compound nouns are everywhere in English.

26
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.820
When you understand them,

27
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.940
you can learn vocabulary much faster because you can figure
out the meaning of a new word by looking at the parts inside

28
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:07.340
it.

29
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.380
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

30
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.800
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Perform Your Own Script.

31
00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.160
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

32
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.700
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

33
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.060
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

34
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.880
You are ready to begin.

35
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.880
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

36
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.600
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

37
00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.680
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
